          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"hamsun-pan-8d02dd8f6f4cc486\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"The highlighted “jeg elsker dig” directly presents the speaker’s love for Eva as true. Its placement in a deliberate list of three loved objects, followed by a comparative question, strongly favors an avowal with truth-conditional force. It does not itself undertake a commitment, and there is no positive evidence that the words emerge as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The speaker’s later claim that the comparison was “only a joke” casts doubt on the sincerity or intended scope of the initial avowal, but not on its dominant truth-presenting mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"The narrator-speaker directly tells Eva “I love you” during their conversation in the forest.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal is embedded in a controlled three-part declaration: he loves a former dream of love, Eva, and the place, then asks Eva which he loves most. Her answer, “The dream,” exposes the emotional problem in his framing. After becoming distressed over another woman and lashing out at Eva, he retracts the comparison, insists that Eva is the one he loves most, and says the earlier statement was a joke. This makes his sincerity and self-understanding unstable, but the highlighted clause itself still functions primarily as an assertion or avowal of love rather than as a commitment or reflexive outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg elsker tre ting, sier jeg så. Jeg elsker en kjærlighetsdrøm jeg hadde engang. jeg elsker dig og jeg elsker denne plet jord.”\",\"supports\":\"The highlighted clause is one element of an explicit, syntactically organized inventory of things the speaker presents himself as loving, strongly supporting T and weighing against E.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Og hvad elsker du mest?” followed by Eva’s answer, “Drømmen.”\",\"supports\":\"The comparative question treats the listed loves as states that can be ranked; it does not frame the highlighted words as an undertaking or commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Later he says: “Det er dig jeg elsker mest; hvordan kunde jeg elske en drøm? Det var bare en spøk, det er dig jeg elsker.”\",\"supports\":\"The later correction confirms that love-avowal is the operative semantic mode while introducing uncertainty about the sincerity or scope of the earlier list.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Before speaking he is described as thoughtful and sorrowful; the utterance is introduced simply by “sier jeg så,” without blurting or comparable loss-of-control framing.\",\"supports\":\"The scene is emotionally charged, but the delivery supplies no independent evidence for expressive/reflexive E force.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a present-tense main-or-embedded explicit love-I-you form in Knut Hamsun’s Pan.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified core occurrence and its ordinary explicit love-avowal construction.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core force as an avowal of a loving state. The surrounding emotional instability, possible strategic framing, and later retraction affect sincerity and interpretation but do not require P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The later “I am yours” has possible commitment force, but it is a separate utterance and should not be imported into the highlighted clause.\"}"
        }
      ],
